<ArticleLayout slug="bc-pnp-ineligible-occupations-employers-2027" datePublished="2026-10-14" dateModified="2026-10-14" articleSection="Guide" keywords="BC PNP ineligible occupations, BC PNP ineligible employers, BC PNP employer requirements 2026, BC PNP 2027, NOC not eligible BC PNP" title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="inline-block bg-indigo-100 text-indigo-800 px-4 py-1 rounded-full text-sm font-bold mb-4 border border-indigo-200 shadow-sm">
        Eligibility Guide
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        BC PNP Ineligible Occupations and Employers: The 2026 Rules and What to Expect in 2027
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        For the first time, the Skills Immigration Program Guide names specific occupations that can never be nominated. Here is the full list, the employer bans that sit alongside it, and how to check your own job offer before you register.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-10-14">October 14, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>13 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">12</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">NOC codes ineligible for every Skills Immigration stream</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-green-600 mb-2">June 13, 2026</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">List applies to applications submitted after this date</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">2 years</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Maximum bar for applicants or employers found to misrepresent</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        On April 23, 2026, the BC Provincial Nominee Program reorganized itself around three objectives, Care, Build and Innovate, and said it would be "expanding the list of ineligible occupations and employers to focus nominations on priorities." The details arrived in the new Skills Immigration Program Guide, effective May 28, 2026 (with a further update dated June 10, 2026). Two changes matter most: a brand-new section 3.11 listing occupations that cannot be nominated under any stream, and an expanded section 6.12 on ineligible employers that now covers organizations providing immigration services.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you hold or are negotiating a job offer in British Columbia, these two sections decide whether your application can succeed at all. A high registration score does not help if the occupation or the employer is excluded. This guide sets out exactly what the current guide says, what it does not say, and what we expect the "expanded list" language to mean as BC plans for 2027.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The 12 Ineligible Occupations (Section 3.11)</h2>

      <p>
        The guide states that "some NOCs are not eligible for nomination under any Skills Immigration stream" and that if your job offer is classified under one of them, "your application will be refused." The list is grouped into three families. The TEER level shown below is the second digit of each 2021 NOC code.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Group</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">NOC</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Occupation</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">TEER</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Administrative professionals</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">12100</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Executive assistants</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Administrative professionals</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">12101</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Human resources and recruitment officers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Administrative professionals</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">12200</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Accounting technicians and bookkeepers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Administrative professionals</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">13100</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Administrative officers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">3</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Administrative professionals</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">13110</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Administrative assistants</td><td class="px-4 py-3">3</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Administrative professionals</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">13111</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Legal administrative assistants</td><td class="px-4 py-3">3</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Administrative professionals</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">13112</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Medical administrative assistants</td><td class="px-4 py-3">3</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Sales and service workers</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">62010</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Retail sales supervisors</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Sales and service workers</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">62020</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Food service supervisors</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Sales and service workers</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">63101</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Real estate agents and salespersons</td><td class="px-4 py-3">3</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Religious occupations</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">41302</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Religious leaders</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Religious occupations</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">42204</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Religious workers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Three details in the wording are easy to miss:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>"Any Skills Immigration stream" means exactly that.</strong> The list applies to the Skilled Worker stream and the Health Authority stream, with or without the Express Entry BC option. A medical administrative assistant (13112) employed by a health authority is still excluded.</li>
        <li><strong>The whole unit group is excluded.</strong> The guide says the restriction "includes all occupations included within the NOC." Every job title that the NOC files under 12100, for example, is caught, whatever your employer calls the role.</li>
        <li><strong>The cut-off is the application date, not the registration date.</strong> The guide notes the list "is in effect for applications submitted after June 13, 2026," and elsewhere says applications are assessed against the criteria in place when the application is received. A registration created before June 13 that leads to an application after it is assessed under the new list.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">Do Not Re-label the Job</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">The BC PNP decides the NOC from the duties in your job description and the evidence in your file, not from the title on the offer letter. Inflating or rewording duties so a bookkeeper looks like an accountant, or a food service supervisor looks like a restaurant manager, is the kind of "altered, overstated or deliberately misleading" information the guide says will not be approved. A misrepresentation finding can bar you, and your employer, for up to two years.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Why these occupations?</h3>

      <p>
        The BC PNP has not published a rationale for each code beyond its stated aim to "focus nominations on priorities." The program's own data shows these job families were already shrinking. In the <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-statistical-report-2025-analysis" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP Statistical Report and Year in Review 2025</a>, "Business, finance and administration" fell from 8.0% of Skills Immigration nominees in 2024 to 5.3% in 2025, and "Sales and service" fell from 12.0% to 4.0%. Our reading is that the list formalizes a shift that was already happening: with a limited allocation, BC is reserving nominations for healthcare, childcare, education, veterinary care, certified trades and high-earning or high-scoring workers, and closing the door on general office, retail and supervisory roles where it judges local supply to be adequate.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Other Occupation Rules That Work Like Exclusions</h2>

      <p>
        Section 3.11 is not the only place an occupation can fail. Several other rules in the guide have the same practical effect:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Rule</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Effect</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Skilled Worker stream is TEER 0–3 only</td><td class="px-4 py-3">TEER 4 and 5 jobs cannot use the Skilled Worker stream. The Entry Level and Semi-Skilled stream that once covered some of them is closed (last invitations December 10, 2024).</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Health Authority stream NOC list</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Direct health authority employees must be in a health occupation (any NOC starting with 3) or in 41300, 41301 or 42201. Physicians, nurse practitioners and midwives have separate rules.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Licensing and registry rules</td><td class="px-4 py-3">NOC 33102 requires registration on the BC Care Aide &amp; Community Health Worker Registry. Unlicensed dental assistants (NOC 33100), such as chairside assistants, are not eligible. NOC 42201 requires a social services or community health diploma or degree.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Drivers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Truck and bus drivers need a valid Canadian licence for the job, and the employer needs a National Safety Code rating of excellent, satisfactory or satisfactory-unaudited.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Self-employment and contracting</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Independent contractor and subcontractor positions will not be approved, nor will jobs where you spend most of your time outside B.C.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        One narrow exception ran in 2026: the Temporary Rural/Remote Health Support Initiative allowed health authority employees in rural or remote areas working as security guards (64410), light duty cleaners (65310) or janitors and heavy-duty cleaners (65312) to register. Its registration window closed on October 7, 2026, and it was described as a one-time initiative for up to 250 workers. Do not plan around it returning in 2027 unless BC announces it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Ineligible Employers (Section 6.12)</h2>

      <p>
        The BC PNP "maintains the discretion to intervene and prohibit any employer" in the situations below. The guide adds a point that surprises many nominees: <strong>previous nominations may be cancelled</strong> if the program later finds the employer met the description of an ineligible employer at the time of nomination.
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Sexually oriented businesses</strong> that produce, distribute or sell pornographic or sexually explicit products, or provide sexually oriented entertainment or services.</li>
        <li><strong>Placement agencies</strong>, employment agencies, staffing firms, professional employment organizations (PEOs) and similar firms. If an agency places you at a client's site, neither the agency nor the arrangement can support your application.</li>
        <li><strong>Organizations that provide immigration advice or services (new in 2026).</strong> If the employer directly employs immigration consultants or lawyers, or its services include immigration advice, it is not eligible. The BC PNP may make an exception at its sole discretion, considering whether immigration is not the employer's main line of business, whether the immigration services are only for internal workforce needs, whether your job and unit are separate from the immigration work, and whether the employer is a large organization.</li>
        <li><strong>Disreputable employers</strong>: any organization, or activity, that "by association would tend to bring the BC PNP or the Government of British Columbia into disrepute."</li>
        <li><strong>Ownership</strong>: in the five years before you apply and throughout the process, you and your immediate and extended family must not hold or control a combined 10 per cent or more of the employer. "Extended family" is broad, reaching first and second cousins of you and your spouse. Acquisitions of a family business by another company are also caught.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">Why the immigration-services ban matters</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">Immigration consultancies and law firms that hire their own international staff, often as administrative assistants or HR officers, are now hit twice: the employer is generally ineligible under 6.12(c), and many of the roles they hire for are on the 3.11 occupation list. Separately, section 2.6 already bars a third-party recruiter who placed you from also acting as your immigration representative.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The Employer Requirements Every Supporting Employer Must Meet</h2>

      <p>
        An employer that is not on the ineligible list can still sink an application if it fails Part 6 of the guide. Use this as a checklist before you register.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Requirement</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">What the guide says</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Established in B.C.</td><td class="px-4 py-3">An ongoing fixed place of business, such as an office, where employees physically report to work.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Business structure</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Incorporated or extra-provincially registered in B.C., or a registered partnership. Health authorities, public post-secondary institutions, school districts, governments, First Nations governments and registered charities or incorporated non-profits are also accepted.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Time in operation</td><td class="px-4 py-3">At least one year operating in B.C.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Minimum staff</td><td class="px-4 py-3">At least five indeterminate full-time employees (or equivalents) working in B.C. inside Metro Vancouver; at least three outside it. Contractors and people working outside B.C. do not count.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Licences</td><td class="px-4 py-3">A valid municipal business licence (or a cited exemption), regulator approval in regulated industries, and a provincial child care licence for employers of NOC 42202 educators.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Compliance history</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Refusal is possible if, within two years, the employer or a director or owner received government or regulatory penalties; within five years, penalties for immigration non-compliance; or if it is under investigation or charged with an immigration or business-related criminal offence.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Local recruitment</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Ads on at least two acceptable locations for at least 14 days, with the operating name, duties, wage, location, contact details and requirements. Language preferences other than English or French, foreign-network requirements and ads tailored to one candidate count against the employer. The requirement may be considered met for senior executives and specialists, or if you already work full-time for the employer in the same occupation.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Genuine need</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The job must support the existing line of business. New lines of business may not be supported, and work normally done by outside contractors may be refused.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Ongoing duty to report</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The employer must notify the BC PNP within 30 days of demotions, terminations, layoffs, extended leaves, delayed start dates, closure or change of ownership.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Section 6.15 leaves a small discretionary door open: the BC PNP may consider an employer that falls short of the minimums if it makes a compelling written business case showing significant economic benefit to B.C., and it may waive some requirements for businesses on First Nation lands or unincorporated lands, and for Indigenous-owned businesses. This is discretionary, not a right, and it does not override the ineligible occupation list.
      </p>

      <p>
        The employer must also complete the Employer Declaration Form in full. The guide warns that vague answers such as "not applicable" or "employee on valid work permit" are treated as incomplete. For a full walkthrough of the paperwork, see our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-employer-guide" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP employer guide</a> and <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-job-offer-requirements-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">job offer requirements</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">A Worked Example: Checking a Real-World Offer</h2>

      <p>
        Consider a candidate working as an office manager for a 20-person dental clinic in Kelowna, with an offer of $31 per hour, permanent and full-time.
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Find the NOC from the duties, not the title.</strong> If most of the work is scheduling, billing, records and supervising reception, the role may fall under 13100 administrative officers or 13112 medical administrative assistants, both ineligible. If the person genuinely plans, directs and controls the operations of the practice, a management NOC might fit, but only if the duties, authority and pay truly support it.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the employer.</strong> A dental clinic is not a placement agency or immigration business. It is outside Metro Vancouver, so it needs at least three full-time employees in B.C., and it must have operated in B.C. for at least a year.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the stream.</strong> A clinic is not a health authority, so this would be a Skilled Worker stream case, which needs two years of skilled (TEER 0–3) experience.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the invitation path.</strong> Even if eligible, invitations in 2026 have gone to Care, Build and High Economic Impact categories. An office role outside those lists would need to compete on wage or points in High Economic Impact draws, which on BC's invitations page ranged from $52 to $70 per hour, or 131 to 138 points, in 2026.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Run your own numbers through the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a> to see how far you are from recent High Economic Impact cutoffs before investing in an application.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What the "Expanded List" Could Mean for 2027 (Analysis)</h2>

      <p>
        The April 2026 announcement used the word "expanding," and the guide delivered that expansion: a 12-code occupation list and a broader employer list. Nothing official says more codes are coming. The following is our assessment, not an announcement.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>The list can change at short notice.</strong> The guide says the BC PNP "makes changes to the program periodically," and new guide editions have appeared twice in 2026 alone (May 28 and June 10). We expect any 2027 changes to arrive the same way: a WelcomeBC news item followed by a guide update with an effective date.</li>
        <li><strong>The allocation drives the pressure.</strong> BC's 2026 allocation is 6,254 after the August 18 increase, and no 2027 figure has been announced. If 2027 brings a similar or smaller allocation, we would expect BC to keep narrowing eligibility rather than widen it. If the allocation grows substantially, the case for more exclusions weakens.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch occupations close to the existing list.</strong> If more codes are added, the likeliest candidates, in our view, are other general office, sales and service supervisory roles with large domestic labour pools. That is a pattern-based guess, not a forecast of specific NOCs.</li>
        <li><strong>Timing protection is limited.</strong> Because the 2026 list applied to applications submitted after a set date, anyone in an at-risk occupation should not assume a registration "locks in" eligibility.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">If Your Occupation Is Now Ineligible</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Confirm the classification honestly.</strong> Read the NOC 2021 description and lead statement for your code and the neighbouring codes. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-noc-codes-teer-categories-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">NOC and TEER guide</a> explains how. Only change your NOC if your real duties match another code.</li>
        <li><strong>A genuine promotion or job change can help, with conditions.</strong> A new role needs a new job offer and, in practice, a new registration. Directly related experience points are scored against the occupation of the B.C. job offer, so a move into a new NOC may cost you experience points.</li>
        <li><strong>Look at federal routes.</strong> The Canadian Experience Class and Express Entry do not use BC's ineligible list. See our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-express-entry-vs-skills-immigration" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry vs Skills Immigration comparison</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Consider other provinces.</strong> Each province sets its own eligibility rules and exclusions. Our <a href="/articles/best-provinces-pnp-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">2027 province comparison</a> summarizes 2026 directions.</li>
        <li><strong>Check your spouse's profile.</strong> If your spouse works in a Care or Build occupation, they may be the stronger principal applicant.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li>Assuming that because you hold a valid work permit in an excluded NOC, you can still be nominated.</li>
        <li>Accepting an offer through a staffing agency that "assigns" you to a client and expecting the agency to support the application.</li>
        <li>Working for a company owned in part by a cousin or in-law and not checking the 10 per cent family ownership rule.</li>
        <li>Letting an employer describe duties that you do not actually perform. The employer signs the declaration, but you are legally responsible for the accuracy of your application.</li>
        <li>Paying for a job offer. In B.C. it is illegal for anyone to ask you for money in exchange for a job; see our guide to <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-job-offer-scams-fraud-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP job offer scams</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Which occupations are ineligible for the BC PNP?</h3>
          <p>Section 3.11 of the Skills Immigration Program Guide lists 12 NOC codes that cannot be nominated under any Skills Immigration stream: 12100 executive assistants, 12101 human resources and recruitment officers, 12200 accounting technicians and bookkeepers, 13100 administrative officers, 13110 administrative assistants, 13111 legal administrative assistants, 13112 medical administrative assistants, 62010 retail sales supervisors, 62020 food service supervisors, 63101 real estate agents and salespersons, 41302 religious leaders and 42204 religious workers.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">When did the BC PNP ineligible occupations list take effect?</h3>
          <p>The program guide states that the list is in effect for applications submitted after June 13, 2026. The list was introduced in the guide edition dated May 28, 2026, following the April 23, 2026 announcement that BC would expand its list of ineligible occupations and employers.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Can my employer give me a different job title to avoid the ineligible list?</h3>
          <p>No. The guide says the restriction covers all occupations within each listed NOC, and the BC PNP classifies a job by its actual duties, not its title. Describing duties inaccurately to fit a different NOC can be treated as misrepresentation, which can lead to refusal and a ban of up to two years for both the applicant and the employer.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Which employers cannot support a BC PNP application?</h3>
          <p>Section 6.12 of the guide makes sexually oriented businesses, placement and employment agencies, staffing firms and professional employment organizations, organizations that provide immigration advice or services, and disreputable organizations ineligible. You also cannot be supported by an organization that you or your extended family have held or controlled 10 per cent or more of in the past five years.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Will more occupations be added to the ineligible list in 2027?</h3>
          <p>BC has not announced any further additions. The April 2026 announcement said the list would be expanded to focus nominations on priorities, and the current guide reflects that expansion. Whether more NOCs are added in 2027 will depend on BC's allocation and priorities, so candidates should re-check each new edition of the program guide.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

